// Regression test for former limitation L13 (REFERENCE.md): `$!!` inside a
// lambda was documented as a silent no-op that did not propagate. Verified
// 2026-06-12: it propagates the error as an early return to the lambda's
// caller — identical semantics to named functions, in both engines.

<# std/io => io

e = io::read("/no/existe.txt")   // soft ##IO error value

// Block lambda: $!! returns the error early, skipping the normal return
h1 = (x -> { x$!! <~ "ok-bloque" })
>> (h1(e))$! ¶                   // → #1 (the error came through)
>> h1("sano") ¶                  // → ok-bloque

// Expression lambda
h2 = (x -> x$!!)
>> (h2(e))$! ¶                   // → #1
>> h2("sano") ¶                  // → sano (non-error passes through)

// Named function (reference behavior — must match the lambdas)
hf(x) { x$!! <~ "ok-fn" }
>> (hf(e))$! ¶                   // → #1
>> hf("sano") ¶                  // → ok-fn

// Inside a HOF: non-error elements flow normally
vals = ["a", "b"]
m = vals$> (x -> { x$!! <~ x })
>> m ¶                           // → [a, b]
